.TH smi-deploy "1" "October 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Base Command"
.SH NAME
smi-deploy \- Upload a package to the servers of an environment and hand it to their deployment service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-deploy
.I upload|handover|deploy
.I PACKAGE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B smi-deploy
puts a package on every server of an environment and hands it to
.BR smi-incoming-deploy (1)
there, which installs it. It is the deploying side of that command, and the only one our projects
need: the
.BR scp ,
the
.BR ssh ,
and the atomic hand-over exist once, here, so that a project's own build file says which package
goes to which environment and nothing else.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B upload
Copies the package into the upload directory, where nothing watches it. Creates the directory,
and lists it afterwards.
.TP
.B handover
Copies the package from the upload directory into the incoming directory, which is what starts
the installation. The package is not read from the local machine, so this works on its own, after
an upload of any age.
.TP
.B deploy
Both, for a caller that wants no step in between.
.PP
The two steps are separate on purpose. An upload takes as long as the line is slow, and
.B setmy-info-deploy.path
reacts to a name appearing in the incoming directory, not to a transfer finishing: uploading
straight into it would install a file that is still arriving. Keeping them apart also leaves the
decision when a machine installs to whoever runs the second step, which is what makes a release
by hand pleasant \- upload now, look at what is there, hand it over when it suits.
.PP
The hand-over copies the package in under
.IR NAME .part ,
which the watcher's
.I *.rpm
glob does not match, and renames it inside the incoming directory. A rename in one directory is
atomic, so the service never sees a package that is still being written. A plain
.B mv
from the upload directory would not do: the two are usually on different file systems, where
.B mv
copies.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.PP
Nothing about any installation is in this toolset: no host, no address, no port, no count of
machines. A CI job configuration or a command line says where a package goes.
.TP
.SM SMI_DEPLOY_HOSTS
The servers, required.
.I [user@]host[:port]
entries separated by spaces or commas, an IPv6 address in brackets as
.B ssh
writes it. One more machine is one more word.
.TP
.SM SMI_DEPLOY_USER
User for an entry that does not name one, default
.BR deploy .
.TP
.SM SMI_DEPLOY_PORT
Port for an entry that does not name one, default 22.
.TP
.SM SMI_DEPLOY_REMOTE_DIR
Where to upload, default the
.I deploy
directory of that user.
.TP
.SM SMI_DEPLOY_INCOMING_DIR
What the deployment service watches, default
.IR /var/opt/setmy.info/incoming .
Set it to
.I /var/opt/setmy.info/incoming/draft
to deploy into the draft stage, which reaches the scriptlets of the package as
.BR SMI_STAGE=draft ;
see
.BR smi-incoming-deploy (1).
.SH "ONE PACKAGE PER CALL"
.PP
A call deploys one package. A set is a call per package, in an order where every state in between
is installable, because the service can start while the rest of the set is still being handed
over: a package that requires another is handed over after it.
.PP
.B smi-incoming-deploy
installs what it finds in one transaction, so a set that arrives together is installed together
and its packages satisfy each other. A package that arrives after the service has started is
installed by the next run, which the watcher triggers by itself.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The package reached every server of the list, and the asked-for actions succeeded there.
.TP
.B 1
No action or no package was given, the action is not one of the three,
.SM SMI_DEPLOY_HOSTS
is unset, a host entry could not be read, the package does not exist for an
.B upload
or a
.BR deploy ,
or a server failed. A server that fails does not stop the others.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Both steps, to one machine:
.RS
.nf
SMI_DEPLOY_HOSTS=lithium.gintra smi-deploy deploy app-1.4.2.rpm
.fi
.RE
.PP
A set of packages, uploaded first and handed over afterwards, to two machines, one of them on
another port:
.RS
.nf
export SMI_DEPLOY_HOSTS='one.example.com two.example.com:27443'
for p in smi-nginx-config-*.rpm smi-nginx-config-test-*.rpm; do
    smi-deploy upload "${p}"
done
for p in smi-nginx-config-*.rpm smi-nginx-config-test-*.rpm; do
    smi-deploy handover "${p}"
done
.fi
.RE
.PP
Into the draft stage:
.RS
.nf
SMI_DEPLOY_HOSTS=live.example.com \\
SMI_DEPLOY_INCOMING_DIR=/var/opt/setmy.info/incoming/draft \\
    smi-deploy deploy app-1.4.2.rpm
.fi
.RE
.PP
What happened on the other side:
.RS
.nf
journalctl -u setmy-info-deploy.service -n 50
ls -l /var/opt/setmy.info/failed
.fi
.RE
